{\fs32\b StockTool - user guide\par}
{\cf3 Everything below is also in the README next to the program. Prices come from Yahoo Finance's public endpoints and are delayed the same way their web site is.\par}
\par
{\fs26\b Quick start\par}
{\b 1.} Click a symbol in the list on the left. The chart, the stats under it and the news pane all follow the selection.\par
{\b 2.} Pick how far back to look with the range buttons above the chart, or {\b Ctrl+1 ... Ctrl+8}.\par
{\b 3.} Add your own tickers with {\b Add... (Ctrl+N)} - search by company name if you do not know the symbol.\par
{\b 4.} Tell StockTool what you own ({\b Ticker > Transactions...} or {\b File > Import from TD...}) and the header, the portfolio strip and the History view come to life. See "Your portfolio, step by step" below.\par
{\b 5.} Not sure what a button does? Rest the mouse on it - every button, the watch list, the portfolio strip and the header have a tooltip.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Watch lists\par}
The column on the left is a watch list. Click a row to select it. Rows show the last price and the day's change (green up, red down); a row turns amber while a price alert is active, and a row with a position also shows the number of shares.\par
\par
{\b Add... (Ctrl+N or Ctrl+F)} opens a dialog with a search box: type a company name or part of a symbol, pick a result and the symbol and name fill in. You can also type a symbol directly. The dialog stays open so you can add several in a row; Close (or Esc) dismisses it.\par
{\b Edit... (F2, or double-click a row)} changes a ticker's symbol, name or currency. {\b Remove} deletes the selected row. {\b Move up / Move down (Ctrl+Up / Ctrl+Down)} reorder the list.\par
\par
Several lists can exist side by side: use the {\b List} menu to create, rename, delete or switch, or click the tabs that appear above the list once there is more than one. The default list cannot be renamed or deleted. Holdings, transactions, alerts and currency overrides belong to the symbol, so they follow it into every list it is in.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Symbols\par}
Symbols use Yahoo Finance notation: {\b RY.TO} for the Toronto Stock Exchange, {\b .V} for TSX Venture, a bare symbol such as {\b AAPL} for NYSE/Nasdaq, and a dash for share classes ({\b BRK-B}, {\b RCI-B.TO}).\par
Canadian mutual funds are not listed under their fund codes: TDB902 finds nothing. Search by name instead ("TD U.S. Index") and pick the fund; its symbol will look like {\b 0P000071WA.TO}. Funds have one price per day, so the 1D/5D ranges are empty and open, high, low and volume show a dash.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Chart\par}
The range buttons (or {\b Ctrl+1 ... Ctrl+8}) pick how far back the chart goes. {\b Candles} switches between a line and candlesticks (each candle shows open, high, low and close for one bar). Volume bars sit under the price. Move the mouse over the chart for a crosshair with the date, open/high/low/close and volume of that bar.\par
The small {\b trend inset} in the corner shows the last five years (or one year, see the config) at a glance; drag it anywhere inside the plot. {\b Compare} overlays every ticker in the list as percentage change over the selected range.\par
Green {\b D} markers on the chart are ex-dividend dates; the hover tooltip shows the amount.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Bench - the benchmark index\par}
{\b What it is.} A benchmark index is a basket of stocks that stands in for "the market": the default here is the {\b S&P/TSX Composite} ({\i ^GSPTSE}), which covers the bulk of the Toronto exchange by market value. It is the yardstick a Canadian portfolio is usually measured against.\par
{\b Why you want it.} A price on its own does not tell you whether you did well. A stock up 8% over a year sounds fine until you notice the index was up 20% - the money would have done better sitting in an index fund. The reverse is just as useful: a stock down 5% in a year when the market fell 15% held up well. The benchmark turns "did it go up?" into "did it beat the alternative?".\par
{\b How to read it.} The {\b Bench} button (or {\b View > Benchmark index}) draws the index as a dashed grey line over the price chart. The index sits around 36,000 while a share price might be 290, so the line is {\b rebased}: it is scaled to start exactly where the stock starts, and only the shape after that matters. The stock line above the dashed line means it has outrun the market since the start of the range; below means it has lagged. Hover anywhere for both percentages side by side, e.g. {\i ^GSPTSE +7.2% vs +11.8%}.\par
{\b Where it applies.} The same dashed line appears in the Portfolio history view, rebased to the first day in view, which answers the same question for everything you own at once.\par
{\b Changing it.} Set {\i benchmark} in the config to any symbol StockTool can fetch: {\i ^GSPC} for the S&P 500, {\i ^IXIC} for the Nasdaq Composite, or an ETF such as {\i XIU.TO} if you prefer something you could actually buy. Leave it empty to turn the feature off - the menu item and button then grey out.\par
{\b One caveat.} Both lines are price-only: neither includes dividends. For a high-yield stock that understates your real return slightly, which is why dividends are reported separately in the holding line and the portfolio strip.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Indicators (View menu and toolbar)\par}
The toolbar across the top of the chart area holds the same toggles as the View menu: Candles, Compare, SMA 20, SMA 50, Bollinger, RSI, the trend inset, the news pane, the benchmark index and the portfolio history. A pressed button means the option is on, and every toggle is remembered between runs.\par
\par
{\b SMA - simple moving average.} The plain average of the last N closing prices, drawn as a line that follows the price. It smooths out day-to-day noise so the underlying direction is easier to see. StockTool draws {\b SMA 20} (about one trading month, orange) and {\b SMA 50} (about one quarter, purple). Price above the average means the trend over that window is up, below means down. The 20 crossing above the 50 is the classic "golden cross"; crossing below is the "death cross". Both lag by construction.\par
\par
{\b Bollinger bands.} The 20-bar average with an envelope two standard deviations above and below it, shaded on the chart. The width of the band is a volatility gauge: it squeezes when the stock is quiet and balloons when it is volatile. Price touching the upper band is stretched high relative to recent behaviour; the lower band, stretched low. Roughly 95% of closes stay inside the bands, so a close outside them is notable, and a tight squeeze often precedes a large move in either direction.\par
\par
{\b RSI - relative strength index (14).} A momentum oscillator shown in its own pane, scaled 0 to 100. It compares the size of recent up-days with recent down-days over 14 bars. {\b Above 70} is usually read as overbought (the run-up has been fast and a pullback is more likely); {\b below 30} as oversold. The dashed lines mark those levels. Divergence is the more useful signal: price making a new high while RSI makes a lower high suggests the move is losing steam.\par
\par
All three describe what has already happened; they are commonly used to time an entry within a view you already hold, not to form the view.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Your portfolio, step by step\par}
{\b Step 1 - tell StockTool what you own.} Three ways, from quickest to most accurate:\par
\par
{\b Ticker > Holding...} - type the number of shares and your average cost per share. Fine if you just want a total and do not care about tax figures.\par
{\b Ticker > Transactions...} - enter each buy and sell with its date, number of shares and price; a sell is a {\b negative} number of shares. StockTool then works out shares held and average cost itself, using the average-cost method (the same as adjusted cost base for Canadian tax purposes), and can also tell you what you realised on sales and which dividends you were actually holding for. Transactions replace the manual holding for that symbol.\par
{\b File > Import from TD...} - the fastest way to get the above right. In WebBroker, export your {\b Activity} (for buys and sells) or your {\b Holdings} (for quantity and average cost) as CSV, then pick the file here. A summary shows exactly what will be written and marks symbols that are not yet in a watch list, with a checkbox to add them. Rows already recorded are skipped, so importing the same file twice changes nothing and you can re-export every month. Symbols are matched automatically ({\b RY} on market CA becomes {\b RY.TO}); for fund codes add a line to the {\i [symbol_map]} section of the config, e.g. TDB902=0P0000A30L. Nothing is sent anywhere and no login or password is involved - it is your own exported file, read from disk.\par
\par
{\b Step 2 - read the header.} With a position, the selected ticker gains a line under its name: shares held, average cost (marked {\b ACB} when it came from transactions), current value, unrealised gain, gain already realised on sales, the day's move in dollars, dividends received while you held the shares, and an estimate of the next twelve months of dividend income.\par
\par
{\b Step 3 - read the portfolio strip.} The panel above the watch list totals {\b the active list} - switch tabs and it follows:\par
{\b Line 1} - total market value, converted into your portfolio currency (CAD by default, {\i portfolio_currency} in the config) using live exchange rates. A leading ellipsis means a rate has not arrived yet.\par
{\b Line 2} - today's move on the left, total gain against cost on the right.\par
{\b Line 3} - estimated dividend income per year, from the last twelve months of ex-dividend amounts times the shares you hold.\par
{\b Line 4} - gains realised on sales and dividends actually received. Both need transactions; a manual holding has no history to compute them from.\par
\par
{\b Step 4 - watch it over time.} {\b View > Portfolio history} (the {\b History} button) swaps the price chart for the value of the whole list over time: a solid line for value, a dotted line for what it cost you, and the benchmark index dashed and rebased to the first day in view, so you can see at a glance whether your money is doing better than the market would have. The range buttons choose the window and hovering shows that day's value, cost, gain and the comparison with the index.\par
The value is recorded {\b once a day}, while StockTool is running and every price and exchange rate is in, to {\b portfolio-history.csv} next to the config file - a plain CSV of date, list, value, cost and currency that you can open in Excel or edit by hand. It only knows about days it was running, so the line starts the day you begin using the feature and there is nothing to draw until the second day.\par
\par
{\b Worth knowing.} Holdings and transactions belong to the symbol, so the same position counts in every list that contains it, while the strip and the history cover the active list only. Cash is not tracked - only the shares you list. If the data provider labels a listing in the wrong currency, set the currency in the ticker's Edit dialog.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Alerts\par}
{\b Ticker > Alerts...} sets a price above and/or below which to be told. When the price crosses a level the row turns amber, the status line says so and a notification pops from the tray icon. The alert re-arms once the price crosses back.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Stats, news and export\par}
Under the chart: open, high, low, previous close, volume, 3-month average volume, 52-week range, market cap, trailing and forward P/E and dividend yield (with the annual amount). {\b View > News pane} adds the latest headlines for the selected ticker; click one to open it in your browser. By default they come from Google News (Canadian edition) searched by the ticker's display name, so a good name such as \ldblquote Royal Bank of Canada\rdblquote  gives better results than a bare symbol; {\i news_source=yahoo} in the config switches to Yahoo's feed. {\b File > Export} writes the watch list or the current chart's bars as a CSV file.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Everything else\par}
Prices refresh on a timer ({\i refresh_seconds}) while a market in the list is open, and every {\i closed_refresh_seconds} (15 minutes by default) once every exchange in the list has closed; the header says {\b Market open} or {\b Market closed} and the status line counts down to the next check. {\b F5} refreshes at any time. {\b View > Theme} picks light, dark or the Windows setting. {\b Minimize to tray} hides the window instead of minimizing it; the tray icon's tooltip lists every price, left-click restores the window, right-click offers Show / Refresh / Exit.\par
{\b Tooltips.} Resting the mouse on any button explains what it does, and the range buttons name their interval and shortcut. The tooltips over the {\b portfolio strip} and the {\b header} are worth knowing about: they list the full set of figures - value, today, cost, unrealised, realised, dividends received and income - which is where to look when the panel is too narrow to show them all.\par
Window size, range, selected ticker, list and every toggle are remembered between runs. The configuration file is {\b stocktool.cfg}: next to the program when run from a folder you can write to, otherwise in %APPDATA%\\StockTool (Help > About shows the path). {\b Reload cfg} re-reads it after a hand edit.\par
{\b Help > Data source health...} shows how each data endpoint (chart, fundamentals, search, exchange rates, news, crumb, TMX fallback) is doing: last HTTP status, latency, time since the last success, consecutive failures and the last error, plus how many fetches are waiting. If Yahoo rate-limits the program (HTTP 429) it backs off that endpoint for one minute, then two, four and eight on repeats; the status line and the health window say when it will retry.\par
When Yahoo cannot supply a chart at all, the same bars are fetched from {\b TMX Money} (the service behind money.tmx.com) as a fallback: daily bars only, so the 1D and 5D ranges stay empty and there are no fundamentals, but prices, charts, the benchmark and the portfolio keep working. The header shows {\b TMX Money (fallback)} while that is the case. Exchange rates for Canadian-dollar pairs fall back to the Bank of Canada's daily average. {\i fallback_provider=none} in the config turns this off.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Keyboard shortcuts\par}
F1 this guide \'b7 F5 refresh \'b7 F2 edit ticker \'b7 Ctrl+N / Ctrl+F add or search \'b7 Ctrl+Up / Ctrl+Down move ticker \'b7 Ctrl+1 ... Ctrl+8 chart range \'b7 Esc closes dialogs\par
